   390	### 4.2 A bottom node
   391	
   392	For (2.2), introduce the allowed cyclic forms for \(g,f\), equate every
   393	nonconstant coefficient of the Wronskian to zero, and equate its constant
   394	coefficient to a variable \(\kappa\). Saturate at
   395	
   396	\[
   397	 U_{\rm bot}=\lambda_g\lambda_f\kappa\Disc(g)\Disc(f)\Res(g,f).
   398	                                                                    \tag{4.6}
   399	\]
   400	
   401	The last three factors are logically redundant when the exact constant
   402	Wronskian and \(\kappa\ne0\) have already been enforced, but retaining them is
   403	a useful audit against a mistaken equation or ring map.
   404	
   405	A particularly small exact reduction applies in the cases below. Let
   406	
   407	\[
   408	 g(\pi)=\pi G(\pi^A),\quad f(\pi)=F(\pi^A),\quad
   409	 \deg F=M,\quad\deg G=N,
   410	\]
   411	
   412	and define the reversed monic polynomials
   413	
   414	\[
   415	 P(w)=w^MF(1/w)=1+a_1w+\cdots+a_Mw^M,\qquad
   416	 H(w)=w^NG(1/w).
   417	\]
   418	
   419	For the convention \(d f g'-e g f'=\kappa\), the Wronskian is constant iff
   420	
   421	\[
   422	 H=\operatorname{trunc}_{N}P^{e/d},\qquad
   423	 h_{N+1}=\cdots=h_{N+M-1}=0,\qquad h_{N+M}\ne0,                \tag{4.7}
   424	\]
   425	
   426	where \(P^{e/d}=\sum h_nw^n\). The coefficients are generated exactly over
   427	\(\mathbf Q\) by
   428	
   429	\[
   430	 h_0=1,\qquad
   431	 h_n=-{1\over dn}\sum_{i=1}^{\min(M,n)}
   432	       [d(n-i)-ei]a_i h_{n-i}.                                \tag{4.8}
   433	\]
