   459	\[
   460	 \mathcal J_{f,g}\times_{\mathcal L_j}X_j
   461	                   \times_{\mathcal L_{j-1}}X_{j-1},           \tag{4.10}
   462	\]
   463	
   464	Here \(\mathcal J_{f,g}\) is the common weighted-jet scheme and
   465	\(\mathcal L_*\) are initial-form maps. They are absent from
   466	\((P,Q,A,b,\{u_i\})\), so (4.10) remains OPEN[SOURCE-JET-GLUING].
   467	
   468	### 4.4 Saturation discipline and controls
   469	
   470	The Singular driver extracts the ideal component in its declared ring:
   471	
   472	    list wrapped = sat(I,ideal(U));
   473	    ideal Isat = wrapped[1];
   474	    ideal G = std(Isat);
   475	
   476	It checks wrapper/type and the normal form of 1. The Padé driver instead uses
   477	\(I+\langle Th-1\rangle\); component mode eliminates \(T\) and maps the result
   478	into a newly declared base ring. Thus neither a sat wrapper nor matching
   479	variable names is mistaken for the intended ideal/ring map.
   480	
   640	\(d_2/(n-M_2)=25/20=5/4\), it is minor. Proposition 5.3 supplies no bottom
   641	child there, so imposing (5.10) on that orbit would be a formalization error.
   642	
   643	**Verdict: PROVED-HERE SURVIVES for the derived diagonal-action projection.**
   644	
   645	### 5.3 Positive and negative saturation controls
   646	
   647	Exact rational saturation gives dimensions \(2,1,2,1,1,1\) for the 64
   648	parent/bottom, 75 parent/bottom, 108 parent, and 90 parent respectively
   649	(parent dimensions 2 retain the q-leader). Zero-ODE-constant controls are
   650	unit ideals. The substantive collision controls \(d=a\), \(b_0=a\),
   651	\(U(a)=0\), and both bottom zero-scale controls are also unit ideals.
   652	
   653	On this host, the complete small Singular batch took 0.02 s and 11,660 KB
   654	maximum RSS; the independent SymPy identity/invariant batch took 0.92 s and
   655	49,332 KB. These are measured reproductions, not complexity estimates.
   656	
   657	### 5.4 Why Appendix II can still kill the controls
   658	
   659	**SOURCE-READ.** For reduced \((16,12)\), pages 208--209 take the major chart
   660	\(\sigma=\pi t^{1/4}\), a minor chart
   661	\[
   662	 \sigma^*=t^{-1}+a_0+a_1t+a_2t^2+\pi t^3,
   663	\]
   664	and a truncation ending in \(\pi t^{11/4}\). A coherent system for the same
   665	\(\bar g,\bar f\) gives a common approximate root \(h\):
   666	\[
   667	 \bar g=h^4+\alpha_1h^3+\alpha_2h^2+\alpha_3h+\alpha_4,\qquad
   668	 \bar f=h^3+\beta_2h+\beta_3.
   669	\]
   670	For \((15,10)\), pages 210--211 similarly use
   671	\[
   672	 f=h^2+2\beta,\qquad
   673	 \beta^2=\alpha h+\gamma,\qquad
   674	 \beta^3=(3\gamma+a)h^2+\varepsilon h+\delta,
   675	\]
   676	plus shared polynomial forms forced by three root subdiscs. These are deeper
   677	common-\((f,g,h,\beta)\) computations, so the local SAT controls do not
   678	disagree with Appendix II.
   679	
   680	## 6. The two requested nonprinted attacks
   681	
   682	### 6.1 Lexicographically first \((90,60)\) excess witness
   954	## 7. Reading: the decorated moment engine and a possible theorem
   955	
   956	### 7.1 Exact root-moment dictionary at one node
   957	
   958	Let \(q\) be monic with distinct roots
   959	\(\beta_1,\ldots,\beta_Q\). Label the root \(\beta_\nu\) by its multiplicity
   960	\(u_\nu\ge0\) in \(p\); \(u_\nu=0\) means a q-only root. Root containment says
   961	\(\sum_\nu u_\nu=P\). Dividing (2.1) by \(pq\) gives
   962	
   963	\[
   964	 \sum_{\nu=1}^{Q}{w_\nu\over\pi-\beta_\nu}
   965	   ={c\over q(\pi)},\qquad w_\nu=P-Qu_\nu.                     \tag{7.1}
   966	\]
   967	
   968	Expansion at infinity yields
   969	
   970	\[
   971	 \sum_\nu w_\nu\beta_\nu^k=0\quad(0\le k\le Q-2),\qquad
   972	 \sum_\nu w_\nu\beta_\nu^{Q-1}=c.                             \tag{7.2}
   973	\]
   974	
   975	For nonmonic \(q\), the last right side is \(c/\operatorname{lc}(q)\).
   976	The \(k=0\) equation is automatic:
   977	\(\sum_\nu(P-Qu_\nu)=QP-QP=0\).
   978	
   979	Equations (7.2), together with distinct-root inequations, are equivalent to
   980	the node ODE. Under a cyclic action, a nonzero \(A\)-orbit contributes zero
   981	to moments whose exponent is not divisible by \(A\). Passing to
   982	\(z=\pi^A\) is therefore exactly the orbit-compressed moment calculation
   983	implemented by (2.4).
   984	
   985	### 7.2 Tree node versus decoration datum
   986	
   987	The whole major tree supplies almost exactly the combinatorial decoration
   988	needed by that local moment engine:
   989	
   990	| Tree datum | Moment/coefficient datum |
   991	|---|---|
   992	| node \(D_j\) | one local ODE variety \(X_j\) |
   993	| \((P_j,Q_j)\) | total p-degree, number of simple q-root slots |
   994	| increment denominator \(A_j\) | cyclic orbit size / quotient coordinate \(z=\pi^{A_j}\) |
   995	| fixed-zero multiplicity \(b_j\) | distinguished q-root \(\beta=0\) with label \(u=b_j\) |
   996	| nonzero factor multiplicity \(u_i\) | common weight \(w_i=P_j-Q_ju_i\) on an \(A_j\)-orbit |
   997	| unused q slot | label \(u_i=0\), weight \(P_j\) |
   998	| major inequality | factor carries a recursive child pointer |
   999	| edge | selected centre, multiplicity, and radius gap |
  1000	| minor factor | terminal label at this resolution; no automatic child ODE |
  1001	| bottom condition (12)/(13) | parity/residue choice of the constant-Wronskian endpoint |
  1002	| Galois orbit | one prototype configuration plus its conjugates |
  1003	
  1004	This dictionary explains why the C_FULL_TREE_PASSPORT screen is natural. For
  1005	weights \(w_i\), a necessary orbit-count bound can be expressed through the
  1006	positive degree
  1007	
  1008	\[
  1009	 d_+=\sum_i\max(w_i,0),\qquad
  1010	 g=\gcd_i|w_i|,\qquad S={Q-e\over A},
  1011	\]
  1012	
  1013	in the form \(d_+/g\ge S\) used by the frozen passport driver. It is a
  1014	node-local necessary condition, not a construction of common global
  1015	coefficients.
  1016	
  1017	### 7.3 Is a uniform sibling-coefficient theorem plausible?
  1018	
  1019	**Plausible in a restricted form.** A local theorem could bound the essential
  1020	dimension/degree of \(X(P,Q,A,\mathbf u)\), equivalently the moment rank after
  1021	translation, dilation, and cyclic symmetry, sharpening the passport screen.
  1022	
  1023	A genuine *sibling* theorem must bound a different quantity: the dimension of
  1024	the image/fiber of the common-jet restriction map
  1025	\[
  1026	 \mathcal J_{f,g}\longrightarrow\prod_{v\in T}X_v.             \tag{7.3}
  1027	\]
  1028	A rank lower bound for shared-jet equalities would upper-bound this fiber.
  1029	The tree lacks the positive-\(t\) coefficients in (3.5), so cannot determine it.
  1030	
  1031	Accordingly:
  1032	
  1033	- the tree-decorated moment engine **is** the whole-tree, node-local
  1034	  ODE/Galois computation;
  1035	- it is **not** the same computation as source-exact sibling gluing;
  1036	- it becomes a sibling engine only after every edge decoration is enlarged by
  1037	  a common-jet transition map.
  1038	
  1039	### 7.4 Single cheapest next experiment
  1040	
  1041	Build one source-faithful edge on Moh's smallest positive control, the reduced
  1042	\((16,12)\) configuration of pages 208--209. Use exactly his common
  1043	\(\bar g,\bar f,h\), the major chart and compatible minor chart, and retain
  1044	only the first weighted jet band needed by formula (3.5) across
  1045	\((\delta_2,\delta_1)=(-1,1/4)\). Clear denominators with one common
  1046	ramified parameter, print the variable/ring map, and compare the extracted
  1047	initial forms and the uniformizer-induced action matrix with the verified
  1048	local families before eliminating.
  1049	
  1050	Cap it at 30 variables, 10 minutes, and 2 GB; if the band exceeds the cap,
  1051	return its counts. Success means reproducing (3.7)--(3.8), deriving the action
  1052	matrix, and identifying one lower child coefficient with a shared jet. Only
  1053	then reuse the generator on 90 or 108.
  1054	
  1055	## 8. Findings ledger
  1056	
  1057	- **SOURCE-READ:** Prop. 4.6 gives squarefree \(q\),
  1058	  \(\operatorname{roots}(p)\subset\operatorname{roots}(q)\), and not-power;
  1059	  A.3/A.4 give \(D=cp\); A.5 gives the coprime constant-RHS bottom.
  1060	- **SOURCE-READ:** Prop. 5.3 gives centre, prefix, radius, multiplicity and
  1061	  degrees, not lower coefficients. Page 201 acts on \(\bar t\); pp.208--211
